{"step_index":399,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T18:43:54Z","input_tokens":35825,"cache_read_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2522,"thinking":"Execution begins according to the approved plan. First step involves creating thesis notes file on the remote server with a local mirror. Next is building and publishing a specific system image on a remote host, followed by registering it in the target location.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"# BlueAgent Empirical Thesis Notes & Benchmark Analysis\n\nThis document compiles the quantitative evaluation results, methodology design decisions, and architectural insights for the thesis on **BlueAgent: Multi-Tenant Cyber Range Verification and Isolation with Incus and Open Virtual Network (OVN)**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. ZFS Copy-on-Write (CoW) Ephemeral Sandboxing (Core Thesis Contribution)\n\n### 1.1 The Challenge of State Verification in Live CTF Infrastructure\nIn Attack-Defense and Red/Blue Cyber Range competitions, teams deploy patches, configuration changes, or service hardening. When the verification engine tests challenge functionality, executing destructive or state-altering verifier scripts directly on the production challenge containers risks corrupting team state or causing cascading outages.\n\nBlueAgent solves this through **ephemeral sandboxing**:\n1. When a team submits or automated health checking runs, BlueAgent snapshots each running container in the team's challenge.\n2. An ephemeral copy of each container is cloned into an isolated sandbox logical network using `incus copy --instance-only`.\n3. Verification scripts execute against the cloned instances in the sandbox.\n4. Upon test completion, the ephemeral clones and isolated OVN network are immediately destroyed.\n\n### 1.2 Quantitative Speedup Factor: Deploy Create vs. CoW Clone\nBecause BlueAgent is backed by a **ZFS storage pool** (`blueagent-zfs`), container cloning uses ZFS Copy-on-Write block pointer references rather than block-level data copies. While creating an initial container instance (`Deploy Create`) requires unpacking and initializing filesystem structures from the base image, cloning a running instance (`CoW Clone`) is an $O(1)$ metadata transaction.\n\nAs system concurrency scales, the performance advantage of ZFS CoW cloning becomes dramatically more pronounced:\n\n| Scenario ($M \\times N$) | Concurrent Containers (Live / Peak Verify) | Deploy Create Avg (`total_create_sec`) | CoW Clone Avg (`total_clone_time_sec`) | **ZFS CoW Speedup Factor** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **$M=1, N=1$ (Baseline)** | 1 / 2 | 2.55s | 1.94s | **$1.31\\times$** |\n| **$M=2, N=2$** | 4 / 8 | 4.86s | 3.51s | **$1.38\\times$** |\n| **$M=2, N=5$** | 10 / 20 | 8.89s | 6.84s | **$1.30\\times$** |\n| **$M=2, N=7$** | 14 / 28 | 11.23s | 8.86s | **$1.27\\times$** |\n| **$M=2, N=10$** | 20 / 40 | 13.78s | 11.73s | **$1.18\\times$** |\n| **$M=5, N=2$** | 10 / 20 | 12.35s | 6.70s | **$1.84\\times$** |\n| **$M=5, N=5$** | 25 / 50 | 20.32s | 11.66s | **$1.74\\times$** |\n| **$M=5, N=7$** | 35 / 70 | 26.28s | 15.05s | **$1.75\\times$** |\n| **$M=5, N=10$** | 50 / 100 | 46.53s | 20.15s | **$2.31\\times$** |\n| **$M=10, N=2$** | 20 / 40 | 27.65s | 7.91s | **$3.50\\times$** |\n| **$M=10, N=5$** | 50 / 100 | 52.67s | 14.17s | **$3.72\\times$** |\n\n### 1.3 Key Architectural Takeaways\n1. **Divergent Scalability Under Peak Load:**\n   - Under heavy multi-tenant concurrency ($M=10$ teams, $N=5$ containers, 100 peak containers running simultaneously), cold instance creation (`Deploy Create`) experiences substantial I/O and process scheduling contention, rising to **52.67 seconds**.\n   - In contrast, ZFS snapshot cloning (`CoW Clone`) completes in only **14.17 seconds**—a **$3.72\\times$ speedup**.\n2. **Metadata-Bound Invariance:**\n   - Because ZFS clones point to existing data blocks until writes occur, cloning latency scales primarily with kernel metadata locks rather than storage I/O bandwidth.\n3. **Implication for Competition Cadence:**\n   - In automated cyber range evaluation ticks (e.g. 2-minute round intervals), full redeployment would consume >40% of the entire tick budget just creating containers. With CoW cloning, the sandbox is ready in ~14s, leaving the vast majority of the evaluation window for actual security checks.\n\n---\n\n## 2. 4-Barrier Synchronized Evaluation Methodology\n\nTo evaluate BlueAgent under rigorous, reproducible conditions, the benchmark suite implements a **4-stage barrier-synchronized architecture**:\n\n```\n[Barrier 1: Parallel Team Provisioning (M Teams)]\n                      │\n                      ▼\n[Barrier 2: Parallel Challenge Deployment (M × N Containers)]\n                      │\n                      ▼\n[Barrier 3: Parallel Ephemeral Verification (M Teams, 2 × M × N Peak Containers)]\n                      │\n                      ▼\n[Barrier 4: Parallel Teardown (M × N Containers + M Teams)]\n```\n\n### 2.1 Why Barrier Synchronization is Critical for Rigorous Measurement\n1. **Elimination of Cross-Phase Noisy-Neighbor Interference:**\n   - Asynchronous systems that mix provisioning, deployment, verification, and teardown introduce high statistical variance: a slow teardown from team $A$ steals I/O bandwidth from team $B$'s deployment.\n   - Isolating each lifecycle phase behind an `asyncio.gather()` barrier guarantees that every team in a tier competes strictly against identical concurrent operations.\n2. **Granular Micro-Phase Telemetry:**\n   - BlueAgent instruments every internal stage via sub-millisecond timestamps:\n     - **Deploy:** Validation $\\to$ Incus Instance Creation $\\to$ Config File Push $\\to$ Start $\\to$ Runtime Network Attachment $\\to$ SQLite Transaction.\n     - **Verify:** OVN Ephemeral Network Creation $\\to$ CoW Clone Creation $\\to$ Network Interface Rebinding $\\to$ Clone Start $\\to$ Verifier Runner Execution $\\to$ Verification Commands $\\to$ Ephemeral Resource Teardown.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Asymmetric Scaling: $M$ Teams vs. $N$ Containers per Team\n\nA vital finding from comparing equal-density workloads ($M=5, N=10$ vs. $M=10, N=5$, both totaling 50 live / 100 peak containers):\n\n| Metric | $M=5, N=10$ (Fewer Teams, Dense Challenges) | $M=10, N=5$ (More Teams, Lean Challenges) | Dominant Bottleneck |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Deploy Wall-Clock** | 56.40s | 68.25s | Host process scheduling ($M=10$) |\n| **Deploy Create Avg** | 46.53s | 52.67s | Incus API daemon concurrent requests |\n| **Verify Wall-Clock** | 29.58s | 26.36s | Clone & boot concurrency |\n| **CoW Clone Avg** | 20.15s | 14.17s | Per-sandbox clone batching ($N=10$ vs $N=5$) |\n| **OVN Network Create Avg** | 1.13s | 2.50s | OVN Central database write lock |\n| **Verify Cleanup Avg** | 3.19s | 3.86s | OVN logical switch deletion |\n\n- **OVN Central Contention:** As the number of independent teams $M$ scales, OVN Northbound/Southbound database transactions become the primary networking factor (`ovn_network_create_sec` increases from 1.13s at $M=5$ to 2.50s at $M=10$).\n- **Per-Team Parallelism:** When $N$ is large ($N=10$), individual team verify calls must snapshot and clone 10 containers, making per-team verification slightly longer (20.15s vs 14.17s), but placing less lock contention on OVN.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Heavy Image (`ubuntu-heavy`) Comparison (Work in Progress)\n\n*(This section will be populated with empirical comparative data upon completion of the Ubuntu 24.04 `ubuntu-heavy` benchmark runs).*\n","Description":"Create thesis notes file detailing ZFS CoW speedup and 4-barrier methodology","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"/home/davidel/.gemini/antigravity/brain/335ea41a-df46-4ffa-bb4c-eefc5a5248cd/thesis_notes.md","toolAction":"Writing thesis notes document","toolSummary":"Create thesis_notes.md"}}]}
